<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bel (god)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bel, meaning 'lord', was a title of the Babylonian god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marduk">Marduk</a> that became the name of a chief god worshipped across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palmyra">Palmyra</a> Bel was the supreme deity, honoured in the great Temple of Bel dedicated in 32 CE and destroyed by Islamic State in 2015, and in Elymais the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> died in 187 BCE while plundering a temple of Bel.</p></div>
